EUROPEAN POWER & GAS LIBERALISATION


The European Parliament and the European Council adopted the Electricity Directive in 1996 and the Gas Directive in 1998. The objective of these legally binding directives is to move away from government-controlled, vertically integrated monopoly markets towards a market structure in which production, transportation, distribution and supply are vertically separated and in which non-network activities are subject to competition.

To facilitate such market structures, the Directives lay down common rules on sector structures, market access, operation and management of activities along the supply chain and interactions between states and markets. 

The table below summarises the key provisions of the Directives and the proposed changes to the Directives (not expected to be in force before Spring 2002).





Current provisions of the Gas and Electricity Directives
EU Commission’s proposals for speeding up reforms
Market opening 
The core aim of the Directives is to force countries to create the necessary conditions for competition, beginning with large consumers. The Directives envisage that competition will affect the following proportion of markets as the size threshhold for eligibility falls:

Gas: 20% by 2000; 28% by 2003; 33% by 2008
Electricity: 26.5% by 1999; 30% by 2000; 35% by 2003
New proposed deadlines are:


All non-residential electricity consumers by 2003
All non-residential gas consumers by 2004
All gas and electricity consumers by 2005
Network access
Gas: regulated or negotiated Third Party Access (TPA)

Electricity: regulated or negotiated TPA, or single-buyer procedure
Regulated Third Party Access (TPA) with published tariffs for all gas and power networks
Regulation
No provision
Independent national regulators with powers of approval over tariffs
Unbundling
Accounting unbundling and “Chinese Walls” protecting commercially sensitive information
Legal and management unbundling of transmission operators

Legal unbundling of electricity distribution operators by 2003 and gas by 2004
Public Service Obligations
Must be clearly defined, transparent, non-discriminatory, verifiable and published
Protection of vulnerable customers’ basic rights, social and economic cohesion, environmental protection, security of supply

Member states to produce annual reports monitoring domestic energy markets
Universal service guarantee for electricity


