BELGIUM GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently open: > 5 mcm; October 2006: All

Independent Regulator: Yes

Production & Storage: Belgium has no indigenous production and is also relatively short of gas storage; large amounts of winter swing available from Gasunie in Netherlands.

Gas industry to see introduction of regulated third-party storage access in June. The move will have little immediate practical effect as Distrigas has no excess capacity to offer.

Consumption: 16 Bcm (1999)

Transmission & Distribution: Distrigas controls transportation activities until independent operator is appointed. 

High-pressure pipeline: the TSO has yet to be designated by the Government. Current access conditions are unclear
Supplying Belgian end users from abroad difficult because of high TPA tariffs charged by Distrigas for distribution within Belgium. Transportation tariffs depend on contract length, such that short-term contracts will be more expensive than long-term contracts.
Very little customer switching

Access: Distrigas has published some access tariffs, however, regulated tariffs are not expected until 2002.

Interconnection: Due to its geographic location, Belgium has become a major transit country, with Zeebrugge as a trading hub. Gas coming from the UK, Norway or Netherlands can be shipped through Belgium to end users in Netherlands, Germany and France.

Interconnector: Belgium is currently operating as a corridor for UK and NL gas (depending on direction of gas flow)

Wholesale trading: Development of spot markets e.g. Zeebrugge, Zelzate





 





DENMARK POWER MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently: >1 GWh; December 2002: All customers (100%)

Independent Regulator: Yes

Production: approx. 80 TWh. 

8 generating companies serving 2 independent systems in Denmark

Production mainly from coal-based CHP and autoproduction
As a result of initiatives to reduce CO2 emissions, no more coal-fired stations will be built; coal-fired units have been closing down and converting to natural gas

Transmission: The Elsam and Elkraft systems are the 2 high-voltage grids in Denmark. Eltra established as the Elsam TSO serving mainland Denmark; Elkraft is the TSO for lines serving eastern Denmark. 

Eltra has proposed to upgrade grid connections to Germany and Norway in an attempt to reduce bottlenecks and to integrate an increasing number of wind turbines onto the network 

Distribution: There are roughly 100 local distribution entities that retain exclusive rights of supply to captive customers within their area

Danish distribution companies propose to abolish current quota system that demands that end-consumers buy a certain amount of power produced in an environmentally friendly manner in favour of a green tariff.

Access: Third party access is regulated

Interconnections: Denmark interconnected with Norway, Sweden and Germany

Total demand: 31 TWh; net exporter (approx 1.5 TWh)
The daily auctions of west Denmark-Germany cross-border hourly transmission capacity has recently begun

Wholesale Trading: Denmark is a member of Nord Pool; high liquidity but impeded by capacity constraints at Denmark-Norway border

Balancing: Since Denmark has no hydro production, balancing is more expensive than in other Nordpool countries. 

Frequency: hourly
Predictability: hard to predict
Cost: balancing during certain hours can be very expensive




 





DENMARK GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently: 30%; January 2008: >35mcm (43%)

Independent regulator: No 

Production & Storage: Dansk Undergrunds Consortium (DUC) produces gas. DANGAS owns storage facilities 

Transmission: DANGAS (owned by DONG) owns and operates the national transmission system and is purchaser of all natural gas from DUC, 60% of which is sold to the 5 regional distco’s, the municipalities of Copenhagen and Frederiksberg and power plants; the remaining gas is exported.

Distribution: Distribution owned by municipalities, the 5 regional distco’s own and manage the low and medium pressure pipeline networks 

Access: Currently no third party access to Danish gas infrastructure

Interconnection: Exports to Germany and Sweden

Wholesale trading: No spot market due to Dangas monopoly





 





FINLAND POWER MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently: all customers 

Independent Regulator: Yes

Production: Approx 68 TWh

Over 100 producers; the largest 2 (Fortum Power/ Heat Oy and TVO) generate 60% of total Finnish supply.  Finnish electricity is generated from nuclear, hydro, and cogeneration. Government has decided to double use of natural gas over next 13 years which implies increase in gas-fired power price spikes in the future

Transmission: Fingrid is the transmission operator. 

Fingrid is to compensate its customers (generators, distributors, suppliers, large industrials) Euro 8m for price spikes in Nordic power market over 2000
Fingrid plans to increase transmission capacity between Finland and Sweden to limit constraints.

Distribution: There are more than 100 distribution companies, mainly owned by municipalities

Access: Third party access is regulated

Interconnections: Interconnections with Norway, Sweden and Russia

Total demand: 76 TWh; net imports are 16% of total demand. 
Fortum has entered into an agreement with the Russian electricity export company TPE on new electricity imports (300MW over 7-year term)


