FRANCE GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently open: > 25 mcm and electricity producers (20%), August 2003: > 11 mcm (28%), August 2008: > 5 mcm (33%)

Regulator: To be established late 2002.  

The French Government has yet to pass a law and associated implementation decrees. EC taking legal action over delay in implementation – law unlikely to be heard by parliament before mid-2002 or come into force before 2003.

Privatisation of GdF delayed until 2002

Production & Storage: Very low indigenous production; 95% of domestic gas consumption is imported: Norway (29%), Russia (27%), Algeria (26 %), and the Netherlands (13%)

GdF owns storage facilities (25% of annual consumption). 
Not all storage is likely to be open to TPA

Transmission: GdF owns 75% of the system. Others are CFM and SNGSO who own and operate their own networks and storage facilities. 

Distribution: EdF and GdF own 95 percent of gas distribution. The rest is in the hands of municipal companies

Access: TPA to be determined – will likely be regulated

TPA access is currently limited to a single entry point (out of five)
GdF has been fairly proactive in establishing transitional TPA tariffs but access conditions have only marginally improved

Interconnection: France is directly connected to Norwegian North Sea sources; Belgian, Swiss and German pipeline systems. It imports Russian gas via Germany and additional Norwegian gas via Belgium. France is a transit country for gas flowing from Norway to Italy and Spain. 

Wholesale trading: There is no spot market for gas

Supply: 

GdF seeking to increase residential and commercial prices by an average of 9.5% effective 1st May – reflects lagged linkage to oil prices.
5% industrial customers have switched from GdF since start of liberalisation




 





GERMANY POWER MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: Currently: all customers

Independent Regulator: No 

Although no regulatory body or policies guide the direction of liberalisation, the government has stated possibility of establishing one if negotiated access fails to improve

Production: Approx 510 TWh

Following recent mergers, production dominated by 5 large companies that all have their own power generating facilities

Approx 50% of generation is coal fired, 35% nuclear

Most generators are still heavily integrated with the distributors 

Transmission: 6 high voltage transmission operators with published access rates.

Distribution: Over 1000 companies operate at the low voltage level although consolidation process well under way.

Access: Third party access is negotiated

Access tariffs are published and unbundled for customers that switch; default customers receive a bundled rate for service
–Costs for service vary dramatically between areas despite similar asset bases – differences of up to 130% 
–Cartel Office has stated that this difference is not related to costs

Cartel Office launched investigation into Edis Energie Nord (majority owned by E.On) over high access charges – Edis forced to reduce charges.

Interconnection: Germany is the biggest power trading area in Europe and has trading links with the Netherlands and Italy (through Switzerland) for exports of power and with France for imports. Germany also trades electricity with Austria, Denmark, the Czech Republic, Poland, Luxembourg, and Sweden.

Total demand: 460 TWh; net imports 0% of total demand.


