GERMANY GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 


Eligibility: Currently: all customers (note this is true in theory but not in practice)

Independent regulator: No. 

Gas companies insist that the current regime of industry self-regulation (“V.V.”) is effective.
European Commission is taking legal action against Germany for failing to implement the gas directive

Production & Storage: 80% imported (Norway, Russia, NL, UK), 20% domestic.  Ruhrgas, Wingas, BEB, Thyssengas and RWE to publish storage tariffs

Transmission: 2 competing high-pressure operators (independent Wingas and incumbent Ruhrgas); in addition, 15 supra-regional operators

Distribution: The local and regional distribution networks are highly fragmented; approximately 700 companies

Increasing concentration amongst utilities parallel to electricity industry

Access: Negotiated TPA under the V.V. 

Amended version of V.V.Gas signed by Germany’s 4 gas associations. V.V.2 expected to come into force in September
Access is difficult – Enron first foreign company to gain access 
Ruhrgas was recently ordered to give Berlin based NatGas access to transport
High transmission prices; significant differences in grid charges; companies seeking access to the gas network face variations of up to 122% in tariffs

WholesaleTrading: 

Hubs in Bunde (NL border) and in Lampertheim, Enron is market maker in both
Plans to develop the German/Dutch trading point of Bunde/Oude into a fully-fledged trading hub (possibly by 1st October 2001)



 




ITALY POWER MARKET OVERVIEW


 




Eligibility: Currently:> 20 GWh (35%); January 2002 > 9 GWh (40%)

