SPAIN GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 



Eligibility: Currently: >3 mcm and power plants and cogenerators; January 2002: > 1 mcm; January 2003: all customers

Independent regulator: Yes

Production and Storage: Spain is 100% dependent on imports. Storage is unbundled but owned mostly by incumbent GasNatural affiliate

Transmission: Owned mostly by ENAGAS S.A. controlled by GasNatural; Enagas is Technical Manager of the System 

Distribution: Unbundled but owned mostly by GasNatural affiliates

Access: 

Regulated TPA; access charges determined by the government
Access to transportation and distribution facilities can only be denied if there is insufficient capacity: take-or-pay obligations may be at risk
TPA decrees to be issued before end 2001

Interconnection: Spain imports gas from Algeria (via Morocco), and Norway (via France). The capacity of both the pipeline to Morocco and the one to France could be doubled if they are upgraded to support higher pressure. Spain also has considerable surplus NLG regasification capacity, which has enabled GasNatural and other players to complement long-term contracts with spot market LNG from Abu Dhabi, Qatar, and Australia 

Supply:

From 2003 no gas company will be allowed to deliver more than 70% of the total Spanish gas consumption
No company will be allowed to have a stake greater than 35% of the ENAGAS share capital.
Marketers have a requirement to keep stocks equivalent to 35 days of forward firm consumption 
Until December 2003, 25% of the Algerian gas will be allocated to suppliers to be sold to eligible customers




 





SWEDEN POWER MARKET OVERVIEW


 



Eligibility: All customers eligible since 1996

Independent Regulator: Yes

Production: Approx. 140 TWh
State-owned companies (49% through Vattenfall) as well as independent producers and municipalities are active in the Swedish power market; the 6 largest producers account for 91% of total generation.

Transmission: Svenska Kraftnat is the transmission operator and owns all high-voltage lines.

Distribution: There are more than 200 distribution companies, mainly owned by municipalities

Access: regulated TPA

Interconnection: In the centre of Nord Pool, Sweden maintains an extensive transmission network and interconnections with neighbouring countries. Sweden is the major power supplier to Finland and Norway and exports power to Denmark and Germany.

Total demand: 126 TWh; net imports 4% of total demand
The recently commissioned 600 MW SwePol underwater cable now gives Sweden access to cheap Polish coal-fired generation


