SWEDEN GAS MARKET OVERVIEW


 


Eligibility: Currently: >25mcm, power plants and cogenerators; 2003: >15mcm; 2006: all custormers

Independent regulator: Yes

Production and Storage: Sweden is 100% dependent on imports, all of which come from Denmark.

Transmission: Vattenfall Naturgas. Note that only a small area in the south of Sweden is connected to the gas grid

Distribution: Vattenfall Naturgas

Access: TPA will be regulated (government proposal – needs change in law). Currently, active players are merely obliged to publish their tariffs.

Interconnection: There is a pipeline connecting Sweden with Denmark

Wholesale Trading: None

Supply:  Gas usage is limited to the south of Sweden, and even in that limited region contributes only about 25% to total energy use.




 




UK POWER MARKET OVERVIEW

 




Eligibility: 100% of customers are eligible

Independent Regulator: Yes

Production: Approx. 350 TWh

Historically, there has been a market power concentration problem in generation, however, recent divestment has reduced concentration slightly
Lifting of gas moratorium

Transmission: The National Grid Company (NGC) runs the transmission grid network.

Transmission auctions proposed by regulator

Distribution:  12 regional electricity companies (REC’s) locally transmit and distribute the power. In Scotland two vertically integrated companies provide transmission and distribution

Ofgem reviewing structure of distribution tariffs

Access: Third party access is regulated

Interconnections: England & Wales connected to Europe with a 2000MW line to France; proposals for interconnections with Nordic countries

Total demand: 320 TWh; net imports 4% of total demand

Wholesale Trading: New Electricity Trading Arrangements introduced in March 2001- Implemented in England and Wales, later in Scotland.  Replacement of pool price with spot bilateral trading and balancing prices

Balancing: Frequency: half-hourly

Predictability: quite volatile
Cost: Unexpectedly high imbalance prices since NETA though settling down.

Supply:  
Renewable power requirement, 
Climate Change Levy imposed on electricity ~ 4.3 £/MWh; 400 renewable generators will be exempt from paying CCL

Consolidation:
  - E.On takeover of generator Powergen 
- Innogy proposed takeover of Yorkshire Power – issues of vertical integration between generation and supply

Around 25% of customers have switched supplier



NB DIFFERENT ARRANGEMENTS EXIST FOR NORTHERN IRELAND




 





UK GAS MARKET OVERVIEW

 



Eligibility: 100% of all customers are eligible

Independent regulator: Yes

Production: North Sea and Irish Sea; Belgium interconnector operational since October 1998 
In 2000, major increase in gas prices - slowing dash for gas fired power stations

Transmission & Distribution: completely unbundled from generation and supply; Transco responsible for both transmission and distribution activities

Auction of entry capacity resulted in clearing prices above actual costs – excess revenue is smeared back via lower transportation tariffs e.g. reduced transportation prices by 15% from 1st June ’01
Further reductions in transportation prices expected for next price control in 2002

Access: regulated TPA

Interconnection: Gas interconnector linking UK and Belgium (Bacton-Zeebrugge interconnector)

The EC to hold an inquiry into operation of UK-Belgium gas interconnector over allegations that North Sea gas producers are exporting excessive amounts of gas from the UK, thereby limiting gas supply in the UK and keeping prices artificially high.

Wholesale Trade: Main trading hubs at Bacton and at the virtual “notional balancing point”, i.e. any point on transmission system. Highly liquid spot markets.

Balancing: Possible reform of gas trading arrangements – regulator proposing a move to hourly rather than daily balancing of shippers’ input and offtake

Supply: 30% customer switching



NB Different arrangements exist in Northern Ireland
